  "led-semantics": {
   "note": "HIG section 10, pages 20-21, transcribed rather than only cited - the citation was here and the tables were not, so nobody could interpret two of these lamps. Each front-panel lamp carries its own vocabulary: GNSS, SYNC and PWR each draw four distinct meanings out of two colours and two behaviours, which is why a state carries a behavior as well as a color. On PWR, Solid Green is \"system power good\" and Blinking Green is \"power good but BMC power fail\"; a model carrying only colour collapses them into one. The management jack is declared per element because its left lamp is green for 1G and its right amber for 10M/100M. The PSU FRU and Fan FRU tables belong to the modules and are transcribed in their own contracts. The HIG gives NO table for the port lamps, so the twenty-four led-arrow, four led-dot and two qsfp-lane-leds placements keep the component default of off/on rather than an invented link/activity vocabulary. Corrected after review: qsfp-lane-leds did NOT carry that default when this was written - it still declared off/link/activity, unsourced, and \"link\" had neither a colour nor a base stylesheet rule so it rendered identically to \"off\". Fixed in common/qsfp-lane-leds v1.2.0"
  },
  "part-numbers": {
   "note": "CORRECTED TWICE, and the second correction reverses part of the first, so both are kept. This device listed `PSU-401-DISB-1` / `PSU-401-AISB` and `FAN-402825-HD` here - FRU part numbers in a field whose schema is \"orderable SKUs for this CONFIGURATION: model -> {part, power-cord}\", meaning the CHASSIS. They were a duplicate too: ufispace/psu-401-dc, psu-401-ac and fan-402825 each carry their own in `attrs.model`, which is where a FRU part number belongs. IT WAS NOT HARMLESS. A DCIM exporter reading the first key as the chassis model filed this router as a device type called FAN-402825-HD in both target libraries, and the S9510-28DC appeared in neither under its own name - see roc-ops/Portrayal#21. A field carrying the wrong KIND of value is worse than an empty one, because every consumer has to guess which kind it got. THE FIRST FIX SAID NO CHASSIS SKU EXISTS AND THAT WAS WRONG. It is a true statement about the HARDWARE INSTALLATION GUIDE - R1.9 names the product `S9510-28DC` in its title, its overview and its dimensions table and prints no orderable number anywhere - and a false one about the world. Two exist and the maintainer supplied them: `S9510-28DC-BN0B` for DC and `S9510-28DC-BR1B` for AC. Searching one document and concluding the fact does not exist is the same error the gaps register records against the XFP bezel opening, made again here. THE TWO DC CONFIGURATIONS SHARE A SKU because they are one chassis differently populated - `dc-populated` fitted optics into the first four SFP28 cages before it was removed (the library ships its models bare by policy, docs/pluggables-design.md decision 2), and populating it was never a different thing to order. AC is, and carries its own. That is exactly the distinction #21 collapses on, and the fold has happened: the single DC entry and the AC entry ARE the two device types this chassis has, one SKU each. The one-SKU point stands - populating a chassis was never a different thing to order, which is why removing `dc-populated` cost no device type. SOURCE IS THE OWNER, NOT A DOCUMENT WE HOLD, and that is recorded rather than dressed up: no UfiSpace PDF held contains either string. If a UfiSpace ordering guide ever lands, it should confirm these two and may add the regional variants the Edgecore SKUs show for the same class of box. than merely coping."
  },
  "qsfpdd-port-numbers": {
   "note": "PAID, by fixing the component rather than the labels. Ports 0-3 keep their numbers at baselines 38.1 and 8.8, where the datasheet front figure puts them: the digit is printed on a coloured band just outside each aperture. The band is faceplate paint - vendor-confirmed - so it is now four rects in panel.decor and common/qsfp-cage@2 is 10.18 tall (0.6 flange + the 9.58 std/qsfp-ganged opening) instead of 13.0. Silkscreen paints over decor and under components, so the numbers now read. The cages for ports 0, 1 and 3 did not move. Port-2 is rotate: 180, so its at: is the box top and shortening the box does not free the digit; its at: was recomputed from the new height and lands at 8.8, which puts its aperture 0.08mm lower than the v1 model had it - 0.4 px at the figure's 4.5 px/mm, and the figure is not self-consistent to better than that: it draws the port-2 digit sitting on the aperture edge rather than clear of it. The band is still NARROWER than the aperture (17.1 against 18.5); it is no longer drawn overlapping it"
  },
